  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe are to minimize the maximum overlap integral C5, which measures how much the function h correlates with its shifted versions. The current record is approximately 0.38085. We need to find a better solution that gets below 0.380876.\n\nThe key insight is that for solutions with higher symmetry, the overlap integral tends to be more balanced but slightly smaller. We will search for a new solution with 12-fold symmetry (12-fold means periodic components repeating every 1/12 of the interval [0,2]). This symmetry helps balance the overlap across many shifts.\n\nOur plan is to: \na) Start from scratch with a new 12-fold symmetric solution\nb) Use a mutation-based optimization approach that respects the symmetry\nc) Use multi-resolution evaluation at several resolutions (n_points = [300, 500, 800])\nd) Compare solutions at different resolutions and select the best\ne) Use an exponential cooling schedule for the temperature parameter in the softmax objective\nf) Include a repair phase to enforce the integral constraint\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe need to find h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] such that:\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- h is 12-fold periodic in the sense of having 12 identical \"blocks\" over [0,2]\n- The overlap integral \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx is minimized for the worst k\n\nIn terms of the discrete version:\n- Let dx = 2.0/n_points\n- The interval [0,2] is divided into n_points points\n- The function h is represented by a vector of length n_points\n- The overlap is given by max_k (dx * np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k])\n- The optimization will use:\n   \u2022 Smoothing temperature T (initially 100 to 10000)\n   \u2022 Targeted repair to reduce worst overlaps\n   \u2022 Block move and swap operations adapted to 12-fold symmetry\n   \u2022 Multi-resolution strategy (start at 300 points, then 500, then 800)\n\nThe new solution must satisfy:\n   0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2\n   The overlap integral improves by at least 0.00005 relative to the current record\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe implementation will have the following stages:\n\nParent Transfer Step:\n1. If initial_h_values is provided, use it as base\n2. Otherwise construct from scratch using 12-fold symmetry\n\nStructure:\n```\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    import numpy as np\n    from scipy.optimize import minimize\n    \n    # Timing setup\n    start_time = time.time()\n    t0 = start_time\n    \n    # Configuration\n    resolutions = [300, 500, 800]\n    max_phases = 8\n    parent_order = ['high', 'medium', 'low']  # which resolution to promote\n    ...\n```\n\nThe detailed implementation must follow these steps:\n\na) Function quantization(s) for evaluation:\n   - Compute the correlation using np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n   - Multiply by dx = 2.0/n_points\n   - Enforce sum(h) = n_points/2 via projection\n\nb) Symmetry-adapted mutations:\n   - Block move: shift entire blocks of 12 samples\n   - Swap: maintain block boundaries by not splitting blocks\n   - Repair: move probability mass from high to low regions to balance overlaps\n\nc) Learning schedule for the temperature parameter:\n   - Start at 1000\n   - Exponential decay every 200 steps: T *= 0.99\n\nd) Multi-resolution handling:\n   - After 30% time, switch to 500 points\n   - After 50% time, switch to 800 points\n   - Keep parent as interpolation of best solution\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nThe algorithm will include:\n\n- Initialization: from scratch with 12-fold symmetry\n- Objective functions: \n  \u2022 smoothmax version\n  \u2022 average of top k overlaps\n- Moves: \n  \u2022 Block moves of 2-15 points\n  \u2022 Swaps within the same block boundaries\n- Enhanced repair: compares original and shifted versions rather than assuming fixed shifts\n\nDefault parameters (sweeping during execution):\n- Budget per resolution: 35% total budget\n- SLSQP iterations: 400\n- Stochastic move rate: 80%\n- Cooling factor: 0.92 per 500 steps\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe algorithm will:\n- Use multi-resolution strategy\n- Keep the best solution throughout\n- Check for convergence\n- Return solution with finite C5\n- Enforce constraint by projection\n- Project to [0,1] range\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal solution must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- compute_c5(h) \u2264 best_found\n- The solution is stored as (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nThe final return is:\nreturn (best_h.values, best_c5.value, best_n_points)",
